=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client/repository feed), <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged + IDisposable state holder, P1/S8) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent view), exactly as the
  sibling W7 admin pages (ApiLogsPage / FeedbackQueuePage / SchemaDriftPage / VehicleCostPage) do. The generated
  prompt's idealized "TeslaSync/Features/Automations/ConditionBuilder.xaml + ConditionBuilderViewModel.cs" is
  mapped onto these real in-repo conventions.
- ConditionBuilder is an UNROUTED web component (web route = (unrouted); it takes the controlled `conditions`
  prop + `onChange`), so — like its sibling automation builder ActionBuilder (feature-views/ActionBuilder.cs,
  also a ContentControl, also unrouted) — it is hosted inside the automation create/edit flow rather than being
  an independently navigated NavigationView destination. There is therefore no standalone NavigationView/back-stack
  entry to register; the "NavigationView registration" allowance in the prompt is N/A for this unrouted surface,
  matching the ActionBuilder precedent. The surface still exposes ConditionBuilderRegistration (slug/title) and
  PII-safe ConditionBuilderDiagnostics (view.opened) for the host (P1/S11).
- Data binding (ADR-004, P1/S8): the manifest data source is useGeofences (GET /geofences). The view-model reads
  through an injected IConditionBuilderSource; the canonical ConditionBuilderSource (ConditionBuilder.Source.cs)
  runs the shared ILocationRepository.GetGeofencesAsync cache-then-network read and maps each raw-JSON emission to
  a typed GeofenceOption list (GeofenceOption.ParseList) while preserving the cache-then-network status. No HTTP
  touches the view.
- States (ADR-006): the geofence dropdown reflects the FULL cache-then-network lifecycle as visible Fluent regions
  — loading (hint "Loading geofences…"), ready (success: prompt-first option list + places), empty (hint "No
  geofences yet …"), stale (Warning chip), offline (Danger chip), error (hint + a Refresh/Retry TsButton). The rest
  of the builder stays interactive throughout; no region is ever left blank. The manifest declares the `success`
  state; loading/empty/error are additionally implemented (and headlessly tested) to satisfy the acceptance
  criteria "loading / empty / error for every data source".
- Panels (ADR-005): each condition is a TsGlassPanel card (Mica/Acrylic glass where the web uses GlassPanel),
  laid out with the W2 design tokens / W3 shared components (TsButton, TsBadge, Caption, the select/input
  primitives) — no hardcoded colors/typography, no direct library imports, no raw HTML.
- i18n (ADR-014): all 19 manifest keys resolve through the ILocalizer facade with the web key names + the web
  default strings verbatim; they are present (with the translation.* prefix) in Strings/en|ar|he/Resources.resw
  (auto-generated by apps/shared/i18n — not hand-edited). The one statePlaceholder literal pattern is the web i18n
  key itself and is marked `// parity:allow` with a reason.
- a11y (ADR-015): every interactive element carries an AutomationProperties.Name (Narrator); the geofence hint is a
  LiveRegion; weekday toggles are ToggleButtons with names; the surface adds no custom motion, so reduced-motion is
  honoured by construction; light/dark ride the token brushes.
- Units (P1/S5): N/A — ConditionBuilder authors comparison thresholds the operator types directly (battery_level,
  etc.); there is no unit-bearing display value to convert. SI is never stored/computed in the view.
- No new dependencies; no backend changes; parity only.

